  "title": "Fill It Up: At the Petrol Station",
  "category": "transport",
  "difficulty": "medium",
  "level": 7,
  "order": 701,
  "theme": "car",
  "culturalNote": "Kenyan petrol stations are full-service: an attendant fills the tank, checks the tyres, and washes the windscreen while you stay in the car. You ask for fuel by amount, not litres (\"weka ya elfu tatu\" = put in three thousand's worth). Polite requests use the subjunctive: \"uweke\" (would you put), \"uangalie\" (would you check), \"nikulipe\" (shall I pay you). \"Naomba\" (I'd like / may I have) is the classic polite opener.",
  "startingPoint": "You pull into a petrol station on Ngong Road. An attendant walks up to your window.",
  "grammarFocus": [
    "subjunctive",
    "polite-requests",
    "object-infixes"
  ],
